On Jul 31, 10:08=A0am, nethack newbie <nethack.new...@gmail.com> wrote:
> 1. =A0What are the priorities for a Barbarian in the early game?

I always play barbies like a beastmaster.  I like to experiment with
pets, and I role-play and have a lot of fun with it.
Barbarian games have led to some of the most interesting and
unexpected situations I've ever encountered in nethack.

> I've been playing a Barbarian off and on for several months now. =A0I
> never seem to get very far, made it to Medusa only once. =A0I know I
> need to change my approach, but am not sure what is most important.

So you're a newbie in general, or a newbie to barbarians?

> 2. =A0When people talk about clearing out the Mines, are they really
> clearing everything and testing all weapons/armor/items for BUC? =A0

Not exactly.  A pet can help considerably with this.  I have been
known to try dozens and dozens of armor items in hopes of finding a +3
helm or whatever.   Weapons that can be used against me, get stashed.
That's really much more important than trying to ID them for my own
use.   For valuable items, I have an extremely complex approach, but I
definitely strive to conserve every resource I can.

> test some stuff when it's convenient, but I get bored trying on that
> 10th pair of iron shoes =A0. . . =A0I read that most people haul stuff up
> to the Minetown altar to test for BUC. =A0Are they really hauling
> everything up?

If that's the only altar, then very likely, eventually, every scroll
and every potion gets hauled there, sure.

> 3. =A0Is it worth trying to keep my exp level low, letting my pet kill
> almost everything, and then buying protection in Minetown? =A0

That's not really a beginner's game.  I would say it is a very tough
challenge to play the protection racket game in particular, and if
you're trying to avoid fights for the purpose of getting to minetown
with minimal gold, that's a losing strategy unless you are prepared
for a very techincal game.  Barbs have one advantage for an early
descent into the mines:  Intrinsic poison resistance.  Don't
underestimate the value of that!

> 4. =A0I'm pretty bad at ID'ing items ... even with spoilers. =A0If the
> item isn't ID'ed easily (writing with a wand), then I usually ignore
> it. =A0Potions, scrolls, rings just kind of take up space in my inv.
> I'm thinking I should diligently try to ID a scroll of identification
> and then start ID'ing the other stuff that isn't easily ID'ed.
> Thoughts?

"Price ID" for scrolls.  There's a process of elimination for most
items.  For a while, the game is all about identifying stuff, IMHO.
Stash stuff so you can stay organized -- you're going to want this
loot later, and if you leave it laying around, it will be used against
you.

Sometimes with items, you'll just have to take a chance.  You can
learn to make educated guesses so that you're taking less of a risk
when you use something.  And then there are last-ditch scenarios where
you pretty much know you're on the last turn of the game unless you
use some item that you hope you've guessed right.


